{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-602",
              "name": "Competitive score forgery in hosted `cups` minigame",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Competitive score forgery in hosted `cups` minigame"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Hosted competitive rooms still queue **`cups`** even though the game exposes enough public state for a client to derive every correct answer and the server accepts a single final client-computed submission. A normal participant can submit a synthesized perfect payload immediately after the round starts and receive the maximum score without legitimately playing the minigame."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Hosted competitive rooms still queue **`cups`** even though the game exposes enough public state for a client to derive every correct answer and the server accepts a single final client-computed submission. A normal participant can submit a synthesized perfect payload immediately after the round starts and receive the maximum score without legitimately playing the minigame.\n\nA low-privilege player can deterministically win any hosted competitive **`cups`** round by forging a perfect final submission before the natural shuffle sequence finishes. This causes unauthorized leaderboard manipulation and score inflation in live rooms; in the validated two-player proof of concept, the cheater received **`5500 pts`** / **`1000`** normalized points while the honest player received **`0 pts`**.\n\n1. Add **`cups`** to `COMPETITIVE_CLIENT_SCORING_DISABLED` so hosted competitive sessions cannot queue it.\n2. Keep `startTest('cups')` and solo practice unchanged so the minigame remains available for local testing and unscored practice.\n3. Before re-enabling **`cups`** competitively, redesign scoring so the server derives results from authoritative interaction data instead of trusting a one-shot client summary payload.",
                "markdown": "Hosted competitive rooms still queue **`cups`** even though the game exposes enough public state for a client to derive every correct answer and the server accepts a single final client-computed submission. A normal participant can submit a synthesized perfect payload immediately after the round starts and receive the maximum score without legitimately playing the minigame.\n\nA low-privilege player can deterministically win any hosted competitive **`cups`** round by forging a perfect final submission before the natural shuffle sequence finishes. This causes unauthorized leaderboard manipulation and score inflation in live rooms; in the validated two-player proof of concept, the cheater received **`5500 pts`** / **`1000`** normalized points while the honest player received **`0 pts`**.\n\n1. Add **`cups`** to `COMPETITIVE_CLIENT_SCORING_DISABLED` so hosted competitive sessions cannot queue it.\n2. Keep `startTest('cups')` and solo practice unchanged so the minigame remains available for local testing and unscored practice.\n3. Before re-enabling **`cups`** competitively, redesign scoring so the server derives results from authoritative interaction data instead of trusting a one-shot client summary payload."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-602",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/602.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-841",
              "name": "Late-joining player can enter the musical-chairs finale and win the session",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Late-joining player can enter the musical-chairs finale and win the session"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The realtime room flow accepts a fresh **`player:join`** during the last queued competitive minigame. Because the musical-chairs finale then seeds participants from **all current room players**, the late entrant can skip the regular game, compete for the **3000-point** finale bonus, and still win the overall session."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The realtime room flow accepts a fresh **`player:join`** during the last queued competitive minigame. Because the musical-chairs finale then seeds participants from **all current room players**, the late entrant can skip the regular game, compete for the **3000-point** finale bonus, and still win the overall session.\n\nAn unauthenticated participant who knows a valid room code can alter the integrity of a live session's final standings and winner selection. In the confirmed proof of concept, a player who joined only during the final queued minigame won the entire session with **3000** points, beating players who had participated from the start.\n\n1. Treat **fresh** joins and **reconnects** as different workflow states, and continue allowing reconnects for existing player identities at any phase.\n2. Reject any new player admission once the room has entered the **last queued competitive minigame**, because the next transition is the bonus musical-chairs finale.\n3. Keep the existing mid-session late-join behavior for earlier minigames only, where the newcomer can still legitimately play later regular games.\n4. Add regression coverage that proves a fresh join during the final queued minigame is rejected while a reconnect for an existing player still succeeds.\n5. Optionally enforce the same rule defensively when building the finale participant list so later workflow changes cannot reintroduce the condition.",
                "markdown": "The realtime room flow accepts a fresh **`player:join`** during the last queued competitive minigame. Because the musical-chairs finale then seeds participants from **all current room players**, the late entrant can skip the regular game, compete for the **3000-point** finale bonus, and still win the overall session.\n\nAn unauthenticated participant who knows a valid room code can alter the integrity of a live session's final standings and winner selection. In the confirmed proof of concept, a player who joined only during the final queued minigame won the entire session with **3000** points, beating players who had participated from the start.\n\n1. Treat **fresh** joins and **reconnects** as different workflow states, and continue allowing reconnects for existing player identities at any phase.\n2. Reject any new player admission once the room has entered the **last queued competitive minigame**, because the next transition is the bonus musical-chairs finale.\n3. Keep the existing mid-session late-join behavior for earlier minigames only, where the newcomer can still legitimately play later regular games.\n4. Add regression coverage that proves a fresh join during the final queued minigame is rejected while a reconnect for an existing player still succeeds.\n5. Optionally enforce the same rule defensively when building the finale participant list so later workflow changes cannot reintroduce the condition."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-841",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/841.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-367",
              "name": "Late forged redemption report can win musical-chairs rounds",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Late forged redemption report can win musical-chairs rounds"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A participant can submit a forged `redemption:report` **seconds after** the server’s `tGreen` and still claim a very low `rawMs`. The server marks the report `flagged`, but still preserves the forged `finalMs`, so the delayed attacker can outrank honest players and manipulate elimination order in the chairs finale."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A participant can submit a forged `redemption:report` **seconds after** the server’s `tGreen` and still claim a very low `rawMs`. The server marks the report `flagged`, but still preserves the forged `finalMs`, so the delayed attacker can outrank honest players and manipulate elimination order in the chairs finale.\n\nA low-privilege participant can **unauthorizedly alter tournament integrity** by eliminating honest players and surviving or winning the musical-chairs redemption round with a fabricated reaction time. This changes bonus placement points and can change the overall winner of the hosted session.\n\n1. Treat **late-arriving redemption claims** the same way as impossible early claims: once `receivedAt` is outside a server-defined arrival window derived from `tGreen`, the claimed `rawMs`, and conservative network slack, convert the result to a disqualified score instead of only setting `flagged`.\n2. Move the late-arrival decision into the central redemption scoring path so every consumer receives a canonical disqualified result rather than a separately flagged-but-still-ranked score.\n3. Preserve `flagged` for operator visibility, but set the stored score to a losing value such as `finalMs: 999999` and a disqualifying status.\n4. Add regression coverage for both the pure scorer and the room-level flow: one test for a delayed forged claim at the scoring layer, and one integration test that proves an honest report beats a late forged submission in `chairs_result`.",
                "markdown": "A participant can submit a forged `redemption:report` **seconds after** the server’s `tGreen` and still claim a very low `rawMs`. The server marks the report `flagged`, but still preserves the forged `finalMs`, so the delayed attacker can outrank honest players and manipulate elimination order in the chairs finale.\n\nA low-privilege participant can **unauthorizedly alter tournament integrity** by eliminating honest players and surviving or winning the musical-chairs redemption round with a fabricated reaction time. This changes bonus placement points and can change the overall winner of the hosted session.\n\n1. Treat **late-arriving redemption claims** the same way as impossible early claims: once `receivedAt` is outside a server-defined arrival window derived from `tGreen`, the claimed `rawMs`, and conservative network slack, convert the result to a disqualified score instead of only setting `flagged`.\n2. Move the late-arrival decision into the central redemption scoring path so every consumer receives a canonical disqualified result rather than a separately flagged-but-still-ranked score.\n3. Preserve `flagged` for operator visibility, but set the stored score to a losing value such as `finalMs: 999999` and a disqualifying status.\n4. Add regression coverage for both the pure scorer and the room-level flow: one test for a delayed forged claim at the scoring layer, and one integration test that proves an honest report beats a late forged submission in `chairs_result`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-367",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/367.html"
            }
          ],
          "version": "1.5.3"
        }
      },
